{'source': 'Qdrant', 'filename': '', 'page': 135, 'chunk_id': 64649, 'score': 0.8019619866176552, 'text': '. Perhaps this is partly because of the nature of the sufferer who, being a smoker, is adjusted to having a chronic cough and does not become aware of a change in the cough until the disease is advanced. A cough is the most common single symptom of lung cancer but other features are shortness of breath, coughing up blood or bloodstained sputum (haemoptysis), chest pain and attacks of chest infection or pneumonia that do not respond completely with treatment. During its early stages, lung cancer causes few problems, so that it is usually not diagnosed until it is quite advanced and usually incurable.', 'rerank_score': 0.9885363578796387}

